==Latin==
{{wikipedia|lang=la}}

===Etymology===
The word ''haruspex'' is composed of {{term||haru-|intestines|lang=la}} and {{term||-spex|lang=la}}, which is derived from {{term|specere|lang=la}}, {{term|specio|speciō|to observe, watch|lang=la}}.<ref>{{R:Nocentini|hw=aruspice}}</ref>

According to Nocentini<ref>{{R:Nocentini|hw=aruspice}}</ref> the first part stems from {{proto|Indo-European|ǵʰer-|intestine|lang=la}}, whence also Latin {{term|hernia||hernia|lang=la}}, and it is cognate to Ancient Greek {{term|χορδή|tr=khordē|lang=grc}}, {{proto|Germanic|garnō|intestines|lang=}} (whence German {{term|Garn|lang=de}}) and to Lithuanian {{term|žarna|žarnà|intestine|lang=lt}}.

The second part of ''haru-spex'' can also be found in the Latin word {{term|auspex|lang=la}}.

===Noun===
{{la-noun|haruspex|haruspicis|haruspicis|m|third}}

# [[diviner]] who reads from the intestines of sacrificial animals; one who practices [[haruspicy]].

